<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Old Quarter</span>
<h1>Tay Ho by Motorbike: Motorbike Rental Planning in Hanoi</h1>
<p>Tay Ho is the district that wraps the western and northern shore of West Lake, known among visitors and expats for its lakeside calm, its international cafes and restaurants, and its slower rhythm compared with the old center. Riders planning time here usually want the same things: a comfortable bike, a rental period that matches the stay, and an honest understanding of the ride between the district and the rental counter in Long Bien.</p>
<p>This planning guide is written by the team at Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, a single verified rental counter at 112 Nguyen Van Cu street, Long Bien district, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00. Riders heading to Tay Ho typically collect a bike from us and ride across the central districts to the lake shore. Start with the <a href="/motorbike-rental-hanoi-old-quarter/">Old Quarter motorbike rental hub</a> for area context, see the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> hub for the full price list, and browse our <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for riding basics.</p>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents"><ol><li><a href="#sec-1">Understanding the District Before You Plan</a></li><li><a href="#sec-2">Choosing a Bike for Tay Ho Streets</a></li><li><a href="#sec-3">Matching the Rental Period to Your Stay</a></li><li><a href="#sec-4">Pickup and Paperwork at the Long Bien Counter</a></li><li><a href="#sec-5">The Ride Between Long Bien and Tay Ho</a></li><li><a href="#sec-6">What a Tay Ho Riding Day Looks Like</a></li><li><a href="#sec-7">Fuel, Weather and Practical Contingencies</a></li><li><a href="#sec-8">A Planning Checklist for Tay Ho</a></li></ol></nav>
<section id="sec-1">
<h2>Understanding the District Before You Plan</h2>
<p>Tay Ho sits around the largest lake in central Hanoi, and its character follows the water. The shoreline brings open riding, temple gates and long views; the streets behind it hold villas, embassies, cafes and the residential calm that drew the district's international community. For a rider, this means the district serves two moods: open riding along the shore and easy, purposeful riding between cafes and shops inland.</p>
<p>The district rewards planning the way any riding district does, with one addition: distances here are more real than in the walkable old center. A day in Tay Ho covers more kilometers than a day in the Old Quarter, so the plan should account for fuel, pacing and honest timing rather than assuming everything is a five-minute hop.</p>
<p>The practical geometry is also worth knowing in advance. Tay Ho lies on the western side of the lake system, so reaching it from our Long Bien counter means crossing the central districts and the bridge, then continuing to the far shore. The return retraces the same structure. Riders who plan around that shape enjoy both the crossing and the district instead of resenting either.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-2">
<h2>Choosing a Bike for Tay Ho Streets</h2>
<p>The district's mix of open shoreline and quiet back streets suits almost every model on our verified list. For simplicity and easy control, the Honda Wave and Yamaha Sirius at 150,000 VND per day remain the straightforward choice, particularly for riders new to Vietnamese traffic who value a light, predictable machine.</p>
<p>Automatic scooters match the district's cafe-to-cafe rhythm well. The Honda Click and Yamaha Mio rent at 150,000 VND per day, and the Honda Vision at 200,000 VND per day adds under-seat storage and a more planted feel on the longer shoreline stretches. The Honda Air Blade, at 200,000 VND per day, suits riders who want a stronger machine for the fuller loop.</p>
<p>Choose by comfort and purpose rather than by power. A rider spending the day between lakeside cafes is well served by any automatic; a rider planning the full shoreline circuit may appreciate the larger frame of the Vision or Air Blade. Tell the counter what your Tay Ho day looks like and let the model follow from the plan.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-3">
<h2>Matching the Rental Period to Your Stay</h2>
<p>A single Tay Ho day runs on the daily rates: 150,000 VND for the Wave, Sirius, Click and Mio, and 200,000 VND for the Vision, Air Blade and an electric motorbike. Collect after the 09:00 opening, ride the shore with unhurried stops, and return the same evening before the 21:00 closing at 112 Nguyen Van Cu.</p>
<p>Visitors often pair Tay Ho with the central districts across several days, and the weekly packages fit that shape: 700,000 VND for the light semi-automatics and scooters, or 1,000,000 VND for the Honda Vision. The comparison is worth running at the counter whenever a trip includes four or more realistic riding days.</p>
<p>For the district's long-stay residents and expats, a monthly arrangement is the natural fit, and Tay Ho is one of the areas where this choice is most common. Monthly rates on our verified list run from 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND for the Wave, Sirius, Mio and Click, 1,500,000 VND for the Air Blade and the electric motorbike, and 1,800,000 to 2,000,000 VND for the Honda Vision.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-4">
<h2>Pickup and Paperwork at the Long Bien Counter</h2>
<p>Every rental we describe starts and ends at the single verified counter at 112 Nguyen Van Cu street, Long Bien, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00. We have no branches in Tay Ho or anywhere else in the city, so a Tay Ho plan should treat the counter as the fixed start and end of the arrangement, with the ride across town planned as part of the trip rather than an obstacle.</p>
<p>Bring your passport or recognized identification, agree the deposit terms at the counter and read the contract before signing. Use the same minutes to confirm the fuel policy, the return time and the process if anything mechanical arises during the rental. The questions cost nothing at pickup and save real time later.</p>
<p>Inspect the bike with the staff before riding off: photographs of its current condition, a check of tires, lights and horn, and a note of the fuel level. The inspection is standard practice for rentals in Vietnam, it takes two minutes, and it makes the return inspection short and friendly for both sides.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-5">
<h2>The Ride Between Long Bien and Tay Ho</h2>
<p>The route from the counter follows Nguyen Van Cu street toward Chuong Duong Bridge, crosses the Red River and continues west through the central districts toward the lake and the Tay Ho shore. The bridge approach is the densest stretch, the central crossing is a genuine city ride, and the final stretches ease as the streets open toward the water.</p>
<p>Ride the crossing defensively and without hurry. A steady line, visible intentions and space around buses and turning vehicles will carry you through the busiest part smoothly, and the district's calmer streets will feel earned by the time you reach them. Most riders find the first crossing the hardest and every later one easier.</p>
<p>We do not quote fixed journey times, because the same corridor flows differently by hour, and honest planning beats invented numbers. Build the day with a comfortable margin in both directions, plan the return against the 21:00 closing, and treat the crossing as the transition it is: the moment when the working city hands you over to the lake district.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-6">
<h2>What a Tay Ho Riding Day Looks Like</h2>
<p>The district supports several day shapes. A shoreline day rides the lake edges of Tay Ho with stops at temples, gardens and viewpoints. A cafe day rides shorter inland legs between the district's many eateries and coffee spots, with the bike parked in between. A combined day mixes both, using the shoreline for the pleasure and the back streets for the errands.</p>
<p>Parking is part of every shape, and the district's arrangements vary from managed lots at the larger stops to informal rows elsewhere. The habit that works here is the same as elsewhere in the city: look for the bike cluster, follow the attendant where one is present, confirm with a nearby shop when unsure and photograph the bike before walking away.</p>
<p>Whatever the shape, the lake remains the district's compass. The water is visible from many Tay Ho streets, and it turns any navigation mistake into a short unplanned sight. Riders who relax into that fact plan lighter days and enjoy them more, which is the entire point of renting in a district built around water.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-7">
<h2>Fuel, Weather and Practical Contingencies</h2>
<p>Tay Ho days cover real distance, so make fuel deliberate. Check the level at pickup, learn where the stations sit along the wider corridors, and top up when passing rather than searching on a low tank. Confirm the fuel arrangement at the counter so the return settles cleanly without surprises.</p>
<p>The district's open aspect means honest weather. The shoreline is exposed to sun and breeze, so the warm months favor morning and late-afternoon legs, shaded midday stops and carried water. Short intense showers arrive quickly and pass the same way; a rain coat under the seat turns them into a pause rather than a problem.</p>
<p>Contingencies stay simple. If rain arrives, shelter and continue; if the day runs long, shorten the loop and keep the best segment; if traffic on the return is heavy, let it be heavy and cross the bridge with patience. A plan with slack bends without breaking, and the district forgives every reasonable adjustment.</p>
</section>
<section id="sec-8">
<h2>A Planning Checklist for Tay Ho</h2>
<p>Before pickup: choose the model that matches your experience, compare daily, weekly and monthly rates against your realistic riding days, prepare your identification and note the counter hours, 09:00 to 21:00 daily at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien. Pin the counter, the lake and your accommodation in an offline map.</p>
<p>At pickup: read and sign the contract, agree the deposit, photograph the bike, check fuel and confirm the return time. Ask the team about the crossing to the western shore and the current general picture in the district; they ride these routes personally and answer without inventing numbers.</p>
<p>On the day: ride the crossing defensively, use the shoreline for pleasure and the back streets for purpose, park thoughtfully at each stop, keep fuel ahead of the tank and return with margin before 21:00. Hand the bike back, settle fuel and deposit openly, and end the rental the way it started, with every term clear.</p>
</section>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h3>Can I rent a motorbike for Tay Ho from your shop?</h3>
<p>Yes. Riders heading to Tay Ho collect a bike at our Long Bien counter at 112 Nguyen Van Cu and ride across the central districts to the lake shore. The counter is open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, so a morning pickup and an evening return fit a single district day comfortably.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h3>Which bike suits Tay Ho best?</h3>
<p>The district's mix of open shore and quiet back streets suits almost every model. The Honda Wave and Yamaha Sirius rent at 150,000 VND per day for light simplicity, the Honda Click and Yamaha Mio at the same rate as automatics, and the Honda Vision or Air Blade at 200,000 VND per day for longer shoreline loops. Choose by comfort and purpose.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h3>How long does it take to ride from Long Bien to Tay Ho?</h3>
<p>We do not quote fixed journey times, because the corridor flows differently by hour. The route is direct: Nguyen Van Cu toward Chuong Duong Bridge, across the river and west through the central districts toward the lake. Plan a comfortable margin, ride the bridge approach defensively and treat the crossing as part of the day.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h3>Is a monthly rental available for Tay Ho residents?</h3>
<p>Yes, monthly arrangements suit the district's long-stay community. Monthly rates run from 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND for the Honda Wave, Yamaha Sirius, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click, 1,500,000 VND for the Honda Air Blade and an electric motorbike, and 1,800,000 to 2,000,000 VND for the Honda Vision. Confirm your model at the counter.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h3>Do you deliver bikes to Tay Ho?</h3>
<p>We operate a single verified counter in Long Bien and do not advertise delivery arrangements. The standard process is pickup and return at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, so plan your Tay Ho time around a ride in from the counter and a ride back before the 21:00 closing.</p>
</article>
<article class="faq-entry">
<h3>What should I check before riding to Tay Ho?</h3>
<p>Inspect the bike with the staff: photographs of its condition, tires, lights, horn and fuel level. Sign the contract, agree the deposit and confirm the fuel policy and return time. Then load an offline map with the counter, the lake and your destination pinned, and check the weather before choosing your day's shape.</p>
</article>
